Problem Context: Hospital Prescription Records

Aim To implement shallow and deep copying in C++ using copy constructors, copy assignment operators and destructors. To ensure that copied prescription objects maintain independent dynamically allocated memory.
Theory A copy constructor creates a new object from an existing object.
Shallow Copy: Copies the pointer address, causing two objects to share the same memory.
Deep Copy: Allocates separate memory and copies the actual data.
Copy Assignment: Copies the contents of one existing object into another.
Destructor: Releases dynamically allocated memory. Rule of Three: If a class requires a custom destructor, copy constructor or copy assignment operator, it generally needs all three.
Algorithm
Start the program.
Create a Prescription class.
Declare an integer and a dynamically allocated string array.
Implement a deep-copying copy constructor.
Implement the copy assignment operator.
Check for self-assignment.
Implement the destructor to release memory.
Create prescription objects.
Modify copied objects independently.
Display the results.
Stop the program.
C++ Program (Complete Code) #include #include using namespace std;
class Prescription { int n; string* medicines;

public: explicit Prescription(int count) : n(count), medicines(new string[count]) {}

// TODO 1: Deep Copy Constructor
Prescription(const Prescription& other)
    : n(other.n), medicines(new string[other.n]) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        medicines[i] = other.medicines[i];
    }
}

// TODO 2: Copy Assignment Operator
Prescription& operator=(const Prescription& other) {
    if (this != &other) {
        string* temp = new string[other.n];

        for (int i = 0; i < other.n; i++) {
            temp[i] = other.medicines[i];
        }

        delete[] medicines;
        medicines = temp;
        n = other.n;
    }

    return *this;
}

// TODO 3: Destructor
~Prescription() {
    delete[] medicines;
}

void set(int i, const string& m) {
    medicines[i] = m;
}

string get(int i) const {
    return medicines[i];
}
};

int main() { Prescription p(2);

p.set(0, "Paracetamol");
p.set(1, "ORS");

Prescription q = p;

q.set(1, "Cetirizine");

Prescription r(1);
r = p;

p.set(0, "Ibuprofen");

cout << p.get(0) << ' ' << p.get(1)
     << " | " << q.get(0) << ' ' << q.get(1)
     << " | " << r.get(0) << ' ' << r.get(1)
     << '\n';

return 0;
}

Expected Output Ibuprofen ORS | Paracetamol Cetirizine | Paracetamol ORS

Explanation

Prescription q = p; calls the copy constructor.
q receives a separate medicine array.
Changing q does not affect p.
r = p; calls the copy assignment operator.
Changing p does not affect r.
The destructor releases the allocated memory when each object is destroyed.
Memory Diagrams Shallow Copy Object p Object q +---------+ +---------+ | pointer |--------------| | +---------+ | | pointer | | +---------+ v Shared Memory +-------------+ | Paracetamol | | ORS | +-------------+
Both objects point to the same memory. This can cause changes to affect both objects and can result in double deletion.

Deep Copy Object p Object q +---------+ +---------+ | pointer | | pointer | +----|----+ +----|----+ | | v v Memory 1 Memory 2 +-------------+ +-------------+ | Paracetamol | | Paracetamol | | ORS | | ORS | +-------------+ +-------------+

Each object has its own independent memory. 8. Three Situations Where Copy Constructor Is Called

When an object is initialized using another object.
When an object is passed to a function by value.
When an object is returned from a function by value, if copy elision does not eliminate the copy.
