Passing an object by value generally invokes the copy constructor. Passing by const reference avoids creating a parameter copy.

Experiment 2: Self-assignment p = p;

The condition this != &other prevents unnecessary memory allocation and deletion.

Experiment 3: Address Sanitizer

Compile using: g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -fsanitize=address -g q2.cpp -o q2

Execute: ./q2

The deep-copy implementation should execute without memory errors.

If the custom copy constructor is deleted, the compiler-generated copy constructor performs a shallow copy. Both objects then share the same pointer, potentially causing a heap-use-after-free or double-free error under AddressSanitizer.

